Subject: [PATCH 10765/11362] Test resolved: second occurrence, and two of my
 own claims were wrong

exp6885 failed its gate with 7 failed checks. document_yaml_task_contract expected
13 tasks and observed document 13, yaml 4. yaml_executable_contract expected ids
6885 through 6897 and observed 6885 through 6888. So the pattern holds two for two:
602 reserved 11 ids and used 4, 603 reserved 13 and used 4, and the id-range reading
is supported rather than refuted.

Two corrections to my own record. First, I reported minutes earlier that exp6885
passed. It logged OK in the conductor log and I read that line instead of the
artifact, whose verdict is complete_blocked_v603_executable_manifest_branch_contract
with gate passed false. The artifact is the authority and the Reading-Results
Discipline exists to prevent exactly this.

Second, the "1 parity failure across 5 replans" base rate I filed an hour ago was
wrong. It came from grepping results for document_yaml_parity, and 603's equivalent
check is named document_yaml_task_contract. Searching the check name rather than the
concept missed a live instance and halved the apparent frequency -- the same
class-B failure, a pattern narrower than the concept, that this file documents
elsewhere. Future counts of this class must match the shape, a document-versus-yaml
task-count comparison, not a literal name.

One difference between the two occurrences is recorded: 602 cascaded and blocked 3
tasks, while 603 has not cascaded yet, so whether 6886 through 6888 block is the next
thing to watch.

Also noted without judging it: the conductor logged OK for a task whose gate failed
seven checks. That may be by design, since complete_blocked_ carries the terminal
prefix and a blocked verdict is honest rather than failed, but the side effect is
that the OK count includes blocked tasks and overstates success.

